WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
Genome editing now maps every cell division from early embryo to organ formation in mammals. This is Planetarian Daily, episode 211. Two independent teams introduced clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats, or CRISPR, barcoding at the single cell stage. One study appears in science and the other in cell. Each round of division is marked, and later tissues keep a readable barcode of the cells they came from. The lineage trees show how individual blast amirs feed

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.160
into the heart, the brain, and the limbs. The result is the first complete map of those early

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.800
blasimir contributions to named organs in living mammals. Two linked papers in the Lancet

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.440
examine major risks to human survival and health systems through the rest of the century.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:50.800
The main analysis comes from the Institute for Health, Metrics, and Evaluation at the University

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.920
of Washington. That paper runs to 59 pages and draws on comprehensive global data assembled

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.660
by the same institute, and accompanying editorial frames the policy implications.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.480
Both pieces appear in the current issue. The editorial addresses how the mapped threats intersect

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:12.600
with existing health, infrastructure, and governance. An international team examined a fossil

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.500
mammal-like animal from the Jurassic, with the findings appearing in nature. Key jaw and throat

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:24.340
bones already show the anatomy needed for suckling, swallowing, and milk consumption. Those structures

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.500
measures predate crown-group mammals by tens of millions of years.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.280
The workplace is mammalian nursing behaviors back in the Jurassic.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.340
Most people assume the jaw and throat structures needed for nursing evolved together with

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.120
live birth in the earliest mammals.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.600
The fossil record points the other way.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.300
That is tens of millions of years before the mammals we now treat as the crown group.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.240
The arrangement lets an infant pull negative pressure inside the mouth.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:54.280
breathing and swallowing have to run as one coordinated sequence while that pressure is held.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.820
The sequence has to work on the first feed, not after a long practice period.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:04.220
Milk intake therefore sits inside a working mechanical loop, not as a later add-on.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.380
Reconstructions of early mammal-like behavior can treat nursing as a plausible activity

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:15.180
rather than an open guess. The next fossils that preserve both the skull and throat soft tissue impressions

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.280
would show whether the full motor pattern was already in place.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.700
Soft tissue traces of that kind would settle the difference between bones that could support suckling

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.320
and a feeding cycle that was actually used.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.540
The 165 million-year timeline also changes the setting in which milk feeding could have been favored.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:37.060
Any selective pressure for nursing would have operated in Jurassic environments.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.440
Those settings were not the habitats occupied by today's placental mammals.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.820
animals. They were not the habitats occupied by today's marsupial mammals either. Researchers

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:52.480
in China, according to a report from Fizz.org, took photosynthetic machinery out of spinach

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.560
leaves and packed it into nanoparticles. The particles were placed into cultured rat heart

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:04.600
cells. Under illumination, the cells generated a denocene triphosphate or ATP. The nanoparticles

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.680
carry thylacoid membranes that run light-driven electron transport inside those mammalian

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.620
cells. Light exposure raised ATP output without forcing the cells to depend only on their own

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.740
mitochondria. The experiment's test whether extra energy production can support stressed

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:26.700
cardiac tissue. The results were reported this week. Stability and non-toxicity in a living

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.480
animal have not been shown. If those conditions hold, the particles could add energy in failing

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.700
hearts where mitochondrial output is already weak. A separate question is whether light can be

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.500
delivered safely to cardiac tissue inside the body.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.760
The particles have been tested only in culture so far.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.560
Performance under the mechanical load and immune conditions of an intact heart is still

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:48.920
unknown.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:54.360
Later experiments would need to show whether the photosynthetic complexes join host membranes,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.340
or stay as free-floating units.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.040
A delivery route that keeps the particles working without damaging nearby tissue has not been established.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.800
Researchers at Florida Tech, according to a report from Fizz.org, measured oxygen at

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.900
more sites across the banana river than any earlier survey.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.340
Low oxygen zones on the river floor send phosphorus and nitrogen back into the water column.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.760
Those nutrients directly support harmful algal growth.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:24.720
The study ties physical oxygen conditions to the chemical drivers of bloom formation.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:30.460
Assistant Professor Austin Fox led the work after finishing his PhD at the same institution

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:36.920
in 2015. The expanded sampling grid caught spatial variation in hypoxia that earlier single-station

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:42.560
measurements had missed. Scientists at NYU Abu Dhabi's Access Center built a model that

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.820
predicts sea ice extent up to nine months ahead with the output appearing in scientific reports.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.300
The algorithm improves on earlier statistical approaches by bringing in extra physical variables.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:59.340
Earlier forecasts were limited to shorter lead times. The model folds in sea surface temperature,

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:04.420
air, atmospheric pressure patterns, and ocean heat content. Validation against historical

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:09.020
records showed better accuracy at lead times beyond six months than prior statistical

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:15.020
baselines. Before we go, whether those spinach-derived complexes join host membranes or stay

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:19.940
free-floating inside an intact heart, is the next experimental question this cultured

